React mein `useEffect` ka cleanup function do khaas moqon par run hota hai:

1. **Component Unmount hone par:** Jab component screen se hatha ya destroy ho jata hai (jaise user ne page change kar diya ho).
2. **Next Effect run hone se pehle:** Agar aapke `useEffect` ki dependency array mein koi value change ho jaye, to naya effect run hone se pehle pichle render ka cleanup function execute hota hai taake purane subscriptions ya timers clear ho sakein.

**Example:**
```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function Timer() {
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      console.log("Timer running...");
    }, 1000);

    // Ye cleanup function hai
    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
      console.log("Cleanup: Timer cleared!");
    };
  }, []); // Empty array ki wajah se ye sirf unmount par chalega

  return <div>Timer Component</div>;
}
```

The error `'Cannot read properties of undefined'` in TypeScript happens when you try to access a property or method on a variable that currently evaluates to `undefined` (it hasn't been initialized or assigned a value).

Here are the most effective ways to fix it:

### 1. Optional Chaining (`?.`)
Use the optional chaining operator to safely attempt property access. If the object is `undefined`, it evaluates to `undefined` instead of crashing.

```typescript
// Before (Crashes if user is undefined)
console.log(user.address.city);

// After (Safe)
console.log(user?.address?.city);
```

### 2. Nullish Coalescing (`??`) or Default Values
Provide a fallback value if the property or object turns out to be undefined.

```typescript
const cityName = user?.address?.city ?? "Unknown City";
```

### 3. Early Return Guard Clauses
Check if the variable exists before trying to use its properties.

```typescript
function printUserName(user: { name: string } | undefined) {
  if (!user) {
    console.log("User is not loaded yet.");
    return;
  }

  console.log(user.name); // TypeScript now knows 'user' is defined here
}
```

### 4. Fix the Initialization
If a variable is typed as `User | undefined`, make sure you fetch or initialize it properly before rendering or parsing its data in your components.

Node.js mein JSON validation ke liye **Zod** sab se behtareen aur modern library hai. Ye TypeScript-first hai, iska syntax bohat clean aur readable hai, aur ye runtime par data validate karne ke liye zabardast hai.

Ek choti si misaal ye hai ke aap Zod ko kaise use kar sakte hain:

1. **Install karein:**
   ```bash
   npm install zod
   ```

2. **API mein use karein:**
   ```javascript
   const { z } = require("zod");

   // Schema define karein
   const userSchema = z.object({
     username: z.string().min(3, "Username kam az kam 3 characters ka hona chahiye"),
     email: z.string().email("Valid email address dein"),
     age: z.number().optional(),
   });

   // Validation function
   function validateUser(reqData) {
     const result = userSchema.safeParse(reqData);
     if (!result.success) {
       // Errors return karein
       return { success: false, errors: result.error.format() };
     }
     return { success: true, data: result.data };
   }
   ```

Agar aapko Joi ya express-validator ke baray mein bhi janna hai jo traditional options hain, toh bataiyega!

Yeh lijiye ek mukammal aur modern Zod schema example user profile ke liye, jismein nested objects, optional fields aur custom validations shamil hain:

```javascript
const { z } = require("zod");

// User Profile Schema
const userProfileSchema = z.object({
  username: z
    .string()
    .min(3, "Username kam az kam 3 characters ka hona chahiye")
    .max(20, "Username 20 characters se zyada nahi ho sakta"),

  email: z
    .string()
    .email("Ghalat email format hai"),

  age: z
    .number()
    .min(18, "Umr kam az kam 18 saal honi chahiye")
    .optional(),

  role: z
    .enum(["admin", "user", "moderator"])
    .default("user"),

  socialLinks: z.object({
    twitter: z.string().url("Valid URL dein").optional(),
    github: z.string().url("Valid URL dein").optional(),
  }).optional(),
});

// Test data ko validate karne ka tareeqa:
const inputData = {
  username: "ali_khan",
  email: "ali@example.com",
  age: 22,
  role: "user",
  socialLinks: {
    github: "https://github.com/alikhan"
  }
};

const result = userProfileSchema.safeParse(inputData);

if (result.success) {
  console.log("Validation kamyaab!", result.data);
} else {
  console.log("Validation errors:", result.error.format());
}
```

Aap is schema ko apni Express API ki requests validate karne ke liye direct middleware mein use kar sakte hain!
